<Base title="About" description="Who builds this notebook, and why their engineering perspective is worth your time.">
	<div class="prose">
		<p class="kicker">About</p>
		<h1>I'm Mirtunjay. I build data systems.</h1>
		<p>
			By day I'm an AVP–Data Engineer at an MNC — Spark, Databricks, pipelines,
			migrations, and the 3 AM pages that teach you what "done" really means.
			By night I build systems of my own: a study platform, a wealth tracker,
			a mutual-fund intelligence system, and the tooling around them. This
			notebook is where that work is recorded — not as a highlight reel, but
			as evidence.
		</p>
		<p>
			Every claim here traces back to something I can point at: code, tests,
			logs, measurements. Where something isn't measured, the page says so.
			Where something broke, the page keeps the failure on record next to the
			fix. I hold outside work to the standard I'd defend in any review, and
			this site is where I prove it — project by project, incident by incident.
		</p>
		<p>
			Lately the work changed shape. AI writes most first drafts now, including
			some of the code on this site. So the notebook changed with it: less
			"here is code," more "here is the reasoning the code came from" —
			constraints, rejected alternatives, verification, and the failures kept
			on record. Implementation is cheap. Judgment is the scarce part, and
			this is where I practice it in public.
		</p>
		<h2>What I work on</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Data engineering — Spark, Databricks, pipelines, migrations</li>
			<li>AI and agents — LLM systems, memory, evaluation, tooling</li>
			<li>Systems and architecture — storage, sync, offline-first design</li>
			<li>Developer tools — scanners, notebooks, automation that compounds</li>
		</ul>
		<h2>How I work, in one page</h2>
		<p>
			Reason first, then build, then measure. Decisions get written down with
			their alternatives. Tests pin the math. Metrics come from runs, not
			adjectives — and the gaps are labeled, not hidden. If you want the
			method stated plainly, open any project page and read its struggle
			section first: that's the notebook in miniature.
		</p>
		<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href="https://github.com/xmirtunjay" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/engineering-notebook/rss.xml">RSS</a>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</Base>

